# What the document says its remainder is, beside the one worked out from its figures. One row per
# layer that both files a remainder and can have one worked out from its own nameplate and demand,
# carrying both figures so a caller can set them against each other.
#
# One pair, several questions
#   This is a relation somebody in this domain would name. `arithmetic/filed_remainder.sqlc`,
#   `matrices.sqlc`, `checks/stated_quantity_is_not_the_magnitude.sqlc` and
#   `checks/fit_domain.sqlc` each need `filed_remainders` joined to `remainder` on (filing, layer),
#   for different questions about the same pair of numbers. The pair is the thing; the questions
#   are the callers'.
#
# What the inner join drops
#   It is an inner join, and the rows it drops are two different kinds of blank. A layer that files
#   no remainder at all is not here (layers/filed_remainders.sqlc keeps `remainder_absent IS NULL`),
#   and neither is one whose demand or nameplate is unstated, so nothing could be worked out. Both
#   are honest, and both are somebody else's question: epistemics/absences.sqlc holds the first,
#   arithmetic/remainder.sqlc the second.
#
# The worked-out figure is the remainder in force
#   The remainder and its magnitude come from layers/remainder.sqlc: worked out from the parts on a
#   composed layer that owes an exact remainder, and from the two totals everywhere else. So a
#   stated quantity is compared with the remainder the model actually holds, and
#   checks/stated_quantity_is_not_the_magnitude can require them equal.
#
# The composed totals are carried too
#   `d_*` and `n_*` are the layer's own composed totals. Where a conversion factor multiplies both
#   the nameplate and the demand of a part, the two converted totals move together, and setting them
#   against each other as if they did not takes the factor's range in twice. matrices.sqlc shows
#   the remainder filed beside the one worked out from those totals, for
#   `merge-holding-composition/compute`: equal at the most likely value, wider at both ends.
-- layers/filed_remainders.sqlc against layers/remainder.sqlc, on the layer they share.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer,
       l.qty_low, l.qty_mode, l.qty_high, l.qty_unit, l.qty_absent, l.qty_derivation,
       r.r_low, r.r_mode, r.r_high, r.unit,
       r.m_low, r.m_mode, r.m_high,
       r.d_low, r.d_mode, r.d_high,
       r.n_low, r.n_mode, r.n_high, r.amount_unit,
       r.sign, r.derived_fit
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/filed_remainders.sqlc)
) l
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/remainder.sqlc)
) r USING (filing, layer)
